Kept the calculation close to the greedy idea. Each item is stored with its density, then the list is sorted from highest density to lowest. An item contributes either its whole value or the value of the fraction that fits.

The comparator uses normal division, which is safe for the JavaScript number values normally used by this template. If the capacity becomes zero, the loop exits early.

```javascript
class Solution {
  /**
   * @param {number[]} val
   * @param {number[]} wt
   * @param {number}   cap
   * @returns {number} maximum achievable value
   */
  fractionalKnapsack(val, wt, cap) {
    const items = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < val.length; i++) {
      if (wt[i] > 0) {
        items.push({
          value: val[i],
          weight: wt[i],
          ratio: val[i] / wt[i]
        });
      }
    }

    items.sort((a, b) => b.ratio - a.ratio);

    let remaining = cap;
    let answer = 0;

    for (const item of items) {
      if (remaining <= 0) {
        break;
      }

      const amount = Math.min(remaining, item.weight);
      answer += amount * item.ratio;
      remaining -= amount;
    }

    return answer;
  }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` from sorting.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the copied item records.

A max heap keeps the item with the best value-to-weight ratio ready at each step. Full items are removed from the capacity first. If the next item is too heavy, only the part that fits is added and the process ends.

A small dry run: capacity `15`, values `[30, 40]`, weights `[10, 20]`. The ratios are `3` and `2`. The first item gives `30`, and the remaining `5` capacity takes one fourth of the second item, adding `10`. The result is `40`.

Zero-weight items are skipped here because the usual input limits use positive weights. The original vectors are not modified.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    double fractionalKnapsack(vector<long long>& val, vector<long long>& wt, long long capacity) {
        priority_queue<pair<long double, int>> items;

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(val.size()); ++i) {
            if (wt[i] > 0) {
                long double ratio = static_cast<long double>(val[i]) / wt[i];
                items.push({ratio, i});
            }
        }

        long double answer = 0.0L;
        long long remaining = capacity;

        while (!items.empty() && remaining > 0) {
            auto [ratio, index] = items.top();
            items.pop();

            long long taken = min(remaining, wt[index]);
            answer += static_cast<long double>(taken) * ratio;
            remaining -= taken;
        }

        return static_cast<double>(answer);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because every item enters and leaves the heap.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the heap.

The item with the largest value per weight should always be used before a lower-ratio item. This implementation finds that item with a simple selection loop, then marks it as used. It avoids a sort and makes the choice visible in the code.

An empty item list or zero capacity naturally returns `0`. The arrays are read-only in practice, so no input values are changed.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public double FractionalKnapsack(int[] val, int[] wt, long cap)
    {
        int n = val.Length;
        bool[] used = new bool[n];
        long remaining = cap;
        double answer = 0.0;

        while (remaining > 0)
        {
            int bestIndex = -1;
            double bestRatio = -1.0;

            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            {
                if (!used[i] && wt[i] > 0)
                {
                    double ratio = (double)val[i] / wt[i];
                    if (ratio > bestRatio)
                    {
                        bestRatio = ratio;
                        bestIndex = i;
                    }
                }
            }

            if (bestIndex == -1)
            {
                break;
            }

            used[bestIndex] = true;
            long takenWeight = Math.Min(remaining, (long)wt[bestIndex]);
            answer += takenWeight * bestRatio;
            remaining -= takenWeight;
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`, since the next best unused item is searched by scanning the array.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the boolean array.

This version does not sort the items. On each round, it scans the unused items and chooses the one with the largest value divided by weight. That is still the greedy rule, just implemented with repeated searches.

The `used` array prevents an item from being selected twice. When the remaining capacity is smaller than the chosen weight, its usable fraction is added and the loop stops.

For `val = [50, 60]`, `wt = [5, 10]`, and `cap = 8`, the first ratio is `10`, so that item is taken completely. Three units remain, and `3/10` of the second item adds `18`, for a total of `68`.

